The editor asked for a pass on pagination and on whether every page looks right (conductor__036). Here is the first one: a new tool, review/tools/print_check.py, builds the full book's PDF as release.yml builds it, in a temporary folder it then removes, and measures each page. This is the list from the build as it stood when I ran it: 132 pages, all 27 chapters located. You fix; I run it again on the next build and tell you what changed.

A chapter's tail alone on a page (the last page of a chapter, less than a quarter full):
  - page 64, end of "The creator comes back": 7 lines, 12% full
  - page 72, end of "Port, not rebuild": 2 lines, 3% full
  - page 86, end of "What did not work": 8 lines, 14% full
  - page 96, end of "A second team, reading cold": 10 lines, 21% full
  - page 129, end of "The words used": 4 lines, 7% full

Pages inside a chapter whose content stops well short of the foot (under 70% full; often a figure or heading pushed to the next page):
  - page 6, in "Slides as a compromise": 49% full, 21 lines
  - page 34, in "Hour three: the five steps, and the first to be caught": 55% full, 23 lines
  - page 39, in "Hours four and five: before dawn": 67% full, 26 lines
  - page 98, in "Sessions end": 60% full, 23 lines
  - page 119, in "The tour, at length": 69% full, 7 lines (a raster figure is on this page)
  - page 126, in "The tour, at length": 53% full, 15 lines

Possible, with low confidence: raster figures on a later page than the paragraph that introduces them, found only in the one chapter where the figure lines and the images could be matched by count:
  - figure on page 5, its introducing paragraph on page 4 (1 apart), in "Slides as a compromise"
  - figure on page 7, its introducing paragraph on page 5 (2 apart), in "Slides as a compromise"
  - figure on page 8, its introducing paragraph on page 6 (2 apart), in "Slides as a compromise"

For information, not a flag: raster figures printed taller than 45% of the text block (the publisher's own warning level) are on pages 5, 7, 13, 60, 113, 114, 115, 119, 120, 121, 122, 123, 124, 125.

What the tool cannot tell, so please judge these yourselves: whether a gap or a short page is meant (a divider, an opener, a page left to a drawing); the figures drawn as vectors, which it can only see through the gaps they leave (24 of 27 chapters have figure lines but no raster images); and how a page looks beyond how far its content reaches. The thresholds are in review/tools/print_check.py and the full result, with page numbers and chapter titles only, is review/anchors/print-check.json.

